      "notes": "All claims are well-tier-matched and quotes entail their claims. The IFS \u00a39bn figure is correctly labelled projected and attributed to full abolition (a conservative bound for the Reform policy). E21's 2023/24 figure is a projection from Commons Library but the claim labels it 'projected to be' so the measurable tier is borderline \u2014 however the 2021/22 outturn figure is genuinely measurable and anchors the claim, so this is acceptable. The ASI sources are correctly flagged as advocacy and weighted accordingly. No party identity or motive attribution. Direction worsens/major is well-supported: even the most favourable (ASI) dynamic scoring leaves a multi-billion shortfall, and the IFS corroborates a large static cost. No false balance issue \u2014 the verdict does not reach mixed/uncertain; it leans with the weight of independent evidence. Lever leakage is not present \u2014 the verdict judges fiscal impact on O12, not stamp duty as a policy in itself."
